---
import Layout from "../components/Layout.astro";
import MetricsStrip from "../components/MetricsStrip.astro";
import CaveatCard from "../components/CaveatCard.astro";
import Plot from "../components/Plot.astro";
import { withBase } from "../lib/url";

const fourArm = {
  cmf: {
    device: "CMF Phone 1 (Nothing A015), Dimensity 7300",
    start: "31 C",
    rows: [
      ["Naive (8 thr, all cores)", "10.8 ± 1.3", "111 ± 13.6", "4,720", "4.25 ± 0.12", "2.61 ± 0.33"],
      ["Threads only (4 thr, no pin)", "15.0 ± 0.5", "137 ± 1.4", "3,803", "4.09 ± 0.17", "3.66 ± 0.24"],
      ["ENTITY Auto (4 thr, pinned)", "18.1 ± 0.4", "139 ± 0.8", "3,739", "3.96 ± 0.07", "4.59 ± 0.17"],
      ["Efficiency (4 thr, LITTLE)", "15.0 ± 0.3", "82.5 ± 0.2", "6,272", "3.51 ± 0.02", "4.28 ± 0.09"],
    ],
    earns: ["+39%", "+21%"],
  },
  oppo: {
    device: "OPPO CPH2729, Snapdragon 6 Gen 4",
    start: "35.3 C",
    rows: [
      ["Naive (8 thr, all cores)", "9.7 ± 0.5", "152 ± 4.5", "3,473", "2.87 ± 1.04", "3.37 ± 1.05"],
      ["Threads only (4 thr, no pin)", "17.4 ± 0.3", "129 ± 22.4", "4,026", "2.52 ± 0.90", "6.80 ± 1.82"],
      ["ENTITY Auto (4 thr, pinned)", "17.5 ± 0.2", "129 ± 23.1", "4,026", "1.78 ± 0.71", "9.85 ± 2.54"],
      ["Efficiency (4 thr, LITTLE)", "14.3 ± 0.1", "127 ± 1.9", "4,101", "3.06 ± 0.96", "4.74 ± 1.57"],
    ],
    earns: ["+80%", "+1%"],
  },
};
---

<Layout
  title="Evidence - ENTITY"
  description="The benchmark method, the four-arm five-run record, the energy result, the competitor comparison with both sessions published, and every limit stated in the open."
  path="/evidence/"
>
  <section data-reveal class="mx-auto max-w-[1200px] px-4 md:px-6 pt-12 pb-8">
    <p class="microlabel">Method first &middot; numbers second &middot; limits always visible</p>
    <h1 class="mt-3 text-2xl md:text-3xl font-bold">The record, with its boundaries</h1>
    <p class="mt-3 max-w-[68ch] text-dim text-[0.875rem]">
      Every number here is measured on a phone, bounded, and traceable to a raw CSV or a named
      file in the source repository. Where two measurements disagree, both are published with
      their dates.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section data-reveal class="mx-auto max-w-[1200px] px-4 md:px-6 pb-10">
    <h2 class="text-xl font-bold">Method</h2>
    <div class="mt-4 grid grid-cols-1 lg:grid-cols-2 gap-4">
      <div class="card p-5 text-[0.8125rem] leading-relaxed space-y-2">
        <p>
          Synthetic llama-bench workload: <span class="font-bold">PP 512 / TG 128</span>, on an
          unplugged phone, one discarded PP 64 / TG 16 warm-up, then the arms in fixed order:
          naive, threads-only, Auto (plus the optional efficiency arm).
        </p>
        <p>
          <span class="font-bold">Thermal control before every pass, in every arm</span> - so
          the ordering cannot favour the last arm: at least 15 s, up to 90 s, until battery
          temperature returns within 0.5 C of the pre-benchmark reading (without waiting below
          37.5 C).
        </p>
        <p>
          Values are the <span class="font-bold">median across runs, ± population standard
          deviation</span>. Every pass exports to CSV: per-pass metrics, 150 ms telemetry
          samples, per-core clock traces, and the per-arm affinity policy.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="card p-5 text-[0.8125rem] leading-relaxed">
        <p>
          <span class="font-bold">The mask the kernel actually applied is logged per arm</span>,
          so a failed <code>sched_setaffinity</code> cannot masquerade as "pinning earns
          nothing":
        </p>
        <pre class="scroll-x mt-3 rounded-[6px] border border-outline bg-bg p-3 text-[0.6875rem] leading-relaxed"><code>adb logcat -s ai-chat | grep "effective cpus"

pinned inference to 8 fast cores, effective cpus [0..7]   &lt;- naive
affinity off, 4 threads placed by the scheduler           &lt;- threads-only
pinned inference to 4 fast cores, effective cpus [4,5,6,7]&lt;- Auto</code></pre>
        <p class="mt-3 text-dim">
          If threads-only and Auto print the same mask, the ablation did not happen and its
          attribution must not be published (benchmarks/REPRODUCIBILITY.md).
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="card p-5 mt-4 text-[0.8125rem] leading-relaxed space-y-2">
      <p>
        <span class="font-bold">What the naive arm is - and why it is not a strawman.</span>
        It runs 8 threads across all 8 cores because that is the number upstream llama.cpp picks
        for itself on this phone. <code>common_cpu_get_num_math()</code> counts only the
        performance cores on hybrid CPUs, but that path is compiled out on Android - the guard
        reads <code>!defined(__ANDROID__)</code> - so the function falls through to
        <code>common_cpu_get_num_physical_cores()</code> and returns 8 on a 4+4 chip.
      </p>
      <p>
        Every multiplier on this site is therefore measured against what an integrator gets by
        building llama.cpp and not passing <code>-t</code>. That is a default, not a
        configuration chosen to lose - and it loses on this hardware for a nameable reason: four
        of those eight threads land on Cortex-A55s at roughly a third of an A78's throughput, and
        every decode step waits on the slowest of them.
      </p>
      <p class="text-dim">
        The honest cost of that choice is that the naive arm is also the noisiest, because a
        straggler thread's timing varies with whatever else the scheduler is doing. It is why the
        July summary is "roughly 2x from the thread count" rather than one fixed percentage, and
        why the naive column carries the widest error bars in every table below.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <MetricsStrip />

  <section data-reveal class="mx-auto max-w-[1200px] px-4 md:px-6 py-10">
    <h2 class="text-xl font-bold">The benchmark of record: four arms, five runs per arm</h2>
    <p class="mt-2 text-dim text-[0.875rem] max-w-[68ch]">
      ENTITY Bench v1.1.0 exports, 2026-07-18, Llama-3.2-1B Q4_0, unplugged, raw CSVs retained
      in <code>benchmarks/results/</code>.
    </p>
    {
      Object.values(fourArm).map((dev) => (
        <div class="mt-6">
          <p class="text-[0.875rem] font-bold">
            {dev.device} <span class="text-dim font-normal">&middot; start {dev.start}</span>
          </p>
          <div class="scroll-x mt-2 card">
            <table class="data-table min-w-[720px]">
              <caption class="sr-only">Four-arm results for {dev.device}</caption>
              <thead>
                <tr>
                  <th scope="col">Arm</th>
                  <th scope="col" class="num">Decode tok/s</th>
                  <th scope="col" class="num">Prompt tok/s</th>
                  <th scope="col" class="num">TTFT ms</th>
                  <th scope="col" class="num">Power W</th>
                  <th scope="col" class="num">tok/W</th>
                </tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                {dev.rows.map((r, i) => (
                  <tr class:list={[i === 2 && "font-bold"]}>
                    {r.map((c, j) => (
                      <td class:list={[j > 0 && "num"]}>{c}</td>
                    ))}
                  </tr>
                ))}
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
          <p class="mt-2 text-[0.8125rem]">
            Thread count earns <span class="font-bold">{dev.earns[0]}</span> decode; pinning
            earns <span class="font-bold">{dev.earns[1]}</span>.
          </p>
        </div>
      ))
    }
    <div class="mt-6 grid grid-cols-1 lg:grid-cols-2 gap-4">
      <Plot
        file="four_arm_decode_20260718.png"
        alt="Bar chart: decode throughput and tokens per watt for naive, threads-only, Auto and efficiency arms on the CMF Phone 1 and OPPO CPH2729"
        regen="python3 benchmarks/plot_four_arm.py"
      >
        The current headline export, both devices, all four arms.
      </Plot>
      <div class="space-y-4">
        <CaveatCard title="What the two SoCs disagree about">
          <p>
            On the Dimensity 7300, pinning buys decode: +21%, with Auto's slowest run (17.5)
            faster than threads-only's fastest (15.9) - non-overlapping distributions. On the
            Snapdragon 6 Gen 4 it buys power instead: decode +0.6% (noise) while median draw
            falls 2.52 to 1.78 W. On both phones the LITTLE-pinned arm loses on speed and on
            tok/W. Per-SoC behaviour, not a universal rule.
          </p>
        </CaveatCard>
        <CaveatCard title="The July record read pinning at ~0%">
          <p>
            The July three-run sets on this same CMF phone measured pinning at -1% to +1%, and
            the 2026-07-15 evening set put the pinned and unpinned medians dead level at 15.9
            tok/s with the pinned spread collapsing from ±1.58 to ±0.09 - repeatability, not
            speed. The five-run exports supersede it; the difference between the two records is
            kept as an open question and the raw CSVs keep it answerable.
          </p>
        </CaveatCard>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section data-reveal class="mx-auto max-w-[1200px] px-4 md:px-6 py-10">
    <h2 class="text-xl font-bold">The July three-run record (history, retained)</h2>
    <div class="scroll-x mt-4 card">
      <table class="data-table min-w-[720px]">
        <caption class="sr-only">July 2026 three-run decode record on the CMF Phone 1</caption>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Model / runs</th>
            <th scope="col" class="num">Naive 8 thr</th>
            <th scope="col" class="num">Threads only</th>
            <th scope="col" class="num">Auto</th>
            <th scope="col" class="num">Thread count</th>
            <th scope="col" class="num">Pinning</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>1B Q3_K_L, 3 runs</td><td class="num">8.8 ± 0.50</td><td class="num">16.9 ± 0.08</td><td class="num">16.7 ± 1.3</td><td class="num">+92%</td><td class="num">-1%</td></tr>
          <tr><td>1B Q4_0, 1 run</td><td class="num">7.9</td><td class="num">14.7</td><td class="num">14.7</td><td class="num">+86%</td><td class="num">+0%</td></tr>
          <tr><td>1B Q4_0, 3 runs (midday)</td><td class="num">7.7 ± 0.78</td><td class="num">15.9 ± 0.22</td><td class="num">16.0 ± 2.1</td><td class="num">+106%</td><td class="num">+1%</td></tr>
          <tr><td>1B Q4_0, 3 runs (evening)</td><td class="num">8.6 ± 0.82</td><td class="num">15.9 ± 1.58</td><td class="num">15.9 ± 0.09</td><td class="num">+85%</td><td class="num">+0%</td></tr>
          <tr><td>3B Q4_0, 1 run</td><td class="num">3.1</td><td class="num">6.0</td><td class="num">6.8</td><td class="num">+94%</td><td class="num">+13%</td></tr>
          <tr><td>3B Q4_0, 1 run</td><td class="num">3.5</td><td class="num">6.3</td><td class="num">6.3</td><td class="num">+81%</td><td class="num">+0%</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="mt-2 text-[0.8125rem] text-dim max-w-[68ch]">
      The two 3B runs disagree, a third taken while charging measured -16%, and single 3B runs
      swing about ±15% - the +13% is noise, not a finding. The honest summary across every
      three-run set is "roughly 2x from the thread count", not one fixed percentage, because the
      naive baseline is the noisiest arm.
    </p>
    <div class="mt-4 grid grid-cols-1 lg:grid-cols-2 gap-4">
      <Plot
        file="decode_attribution.png"
        alt="Decode attribution chart across the July three-run sets: the thread count carries the multiplier, pinning is near zero"
        regen="python3 benchmarks/plot_results.py"
      >
        The July attribution: thread count carries it; pinning ~0% in this record.
      </Plot>
      <Plot
        file="contributed_multidevice.png"
        alt="Contributed multi-device chart: naive to threads-only and threads-only to optimized steps per device"
        regen="python3 benchmarks/plot_contributed.py"
      >
        The contributed dataset's two steps per device - the thread step generalises, the pin
        step is device-dependent.
      </Plot>
    </div>
  </section>

  <MetricsStrip />

  <section data-reveal class="mx-auto max-w-[1200px] px-4 md:px-6 py-10">
    <h2 class="text-xl font-bold">Energy: the same work, 42-47% less battery</h2>
    <div class="mt-4 grid grid-cols-1 lg:grid-cols-2 gap-4 items-start">
      <div class="text-[0.8125rem] leading-relaxed space-y-3">
        <p>
          The CSV export records battery current every 150 ms, so the energy a pass cost is
          integrated from the measured power curve (trapezoid over 273 samples), not inferred.
          From the 2026-07-15 evening export, first pass of each arm:
        </p>
        <div class="scroll-x card">
          <table class="data-table min-w-[420px]">
            <caption class="sr-only">Energy for 128 tokens per arm</caption>
            <thead>
              <tr><th scope="col">Arm</th><th scope="col" class="num">Duration</th><th scope="col" class="num">Mean power</th><th scope="col" class="num">Energy, 128 tok</th></tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td>Naive (8 threads)</td><td class="num">19.9 s</td><td class="num">4.34 W</td><td class="num">86 J</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Threads only</td><td class="num">12.2 s</td><td class="num">3.98 W</td><td class="num">49 J (-44%)</td></tr>
              <tr class="font-bold"><td>ENTITY Auto</td><td class="num">11.8 s</td><td class="num">4.22 W</td><td class="num">50 J (-42%)</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p>
          All three configurations draw roughly the same watts. ENTITY does not win by sipping
          less current - <span class="font-bold">it wins because it finishes in 11.8 s instead
          of 19.9 s</span>. The earlier 2026-07-14 single-run export measured the same shape:
          95 / 57 / 51 J. Threads-only vs Auto is inside the noise on this metric.
        </p>
        <CaveatCard title="Boundary">
          <p>
            Battery current reporting is OEM-dependent: comparative on one device, not lab-grade
            metering. The energy script refuses to run on a charging export, because the current
            would be the charger's rather than the workload's.
          </p>
        </CaveatCard>
      </div>
      <div class="space-y-4">
        <Plot
          file="energy_per_task.png"
          alt="Energy per 128-token task per arm, integrated from the measured power curve"
          regen="python3 benchmarks/plot_energy.py benchmarks/results/entity_1b-q4_0_unplugged_3run_20260715b.csv"
        >
          Energy is the area under the power curve - the left panel is a literal picture of the
          right one.
        </Plot>
        <Plot
          file="energy_efficiency.png"
          alt="Tokens per watt comparison chart"
          regen="python3 benchmarks/plot_results.py"
        >
          Tokens per watt across configurations.
        </Plot>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="equivalence" data-reveal class="mx-auto max-w-[1200px] px-4 md:px-6 py-10 scroll-mt-20">
    <h2 class="text-xl font-bold">The speedup does not change the answer</h2>
    <div class="mt-4 grid grid-cols-1 lg:grid-cols-2 gap-4 items-start">
      <div class="text-[0.8125rem] leading-relaxed space-y-3">
        <p>
          Every speed number on this site comes from changing how work is <em>scheduled</em> -
          how many threads decode uses, which cores they run on. Nothing touches the weights or
          the sampler, so the obvious assumption is that only the wait gets shorter.
        </p>
        <p>
          That assumption is not free. ggml splits a row's dot product across threads and sums
          the partials, so <span class="font-bold">the thread count fixes the order of a
          floating-point reduction</span> - and floating-point addition is not associative.
          Different order, different last bits; different last bits in a logit, and a near-tie
          token flips. This project already depends on the effect being real: the perplexity
          sweep pins its thread count for exactly this reason. It had never been measured.
        </p>
        <div class="scroll-x card">
          <table class="data-table min-w-[520px]">
            <caption class="sr-only">Output equivalence across scheduling arms</caption>
            <thead>
              <tr><th scope="col">Arm</th><th scope="col" class="num">Threads</th><th scope="col">CPUs</th><th scope="col">96 greedy tokens</th><th scope="col">Per-chunk PPL</th></tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td>Naive</td><td class="num">8</td><td>0-7</td><td>byte-identical</td><td>identical 12/12</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Threads only</td><td class="num">4</td><td>0-7</td><td>byte-identical</td><td>identical 12/12</td></tr>
              <tr class="font-bold"><td>ENTITY Auto</td><td class="num">4</td><td>4-7</td><td>reference</td><td>reference</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Efficiency</td><td class="num">4</td><td>0-3</td><td>byte-identical</td><td>identical 12/12</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p>
          Dimensity 7300, Llama-3.2-1B Q4_0, greedy decoding at a fixed seed, perplexity over 12
          chunks of wikitext-2. <span class="font-bold">Naive against Auto is the pair that
          matters</span> - the only comparison where the reduction width actually changes, and
          also the baseline the decode gain is measured from. Same 96 tokens byte for byte, same
          twelve per-chunk values, final PPL 19.2128 on all four arms.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="space-y-4">
        <div class="text-[0.8125rem] leading-relaxed space-y-3">
          <p class="font-bold">Three controls, because a broken test also prints "identical"</p>
          <div class="scroll-x card">
            <table class="data-table min-w-[420px]">
              <caption class="sr-only">Controls for the equivalence measurement</caption>
              <thead>
                <tr><th scope="col">Control</th><th scope="col">Must</th><th scope="col">Observed</th></tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                <tr><td>Reference arm, run twice</td><td>match</td><td>identical</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Reference arm, one word added to the prompt</td><td>differ</td><td>differs</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Reference arm over 3 chunks, not 12</td><td>differ</td><td>differs</td></tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
          <p>
            The third control is what makes the perplexity column mean anything, and it was found
            rather than designed. A 3-chunk run scores chunk 1 at <code>8.3443</code> where the
            12-chunk run scores <code>8.3423</code> - same 512 tokens, same file, same 4 threads
            on the same cluster. Perplexity packs chunks into one batch, so the chunk total sets
            how many sequences are scored together and therefore the shape of the matmuls doing
            it. Three sequences in a batch accumulate differently from four.
          </p>
          <p>
            So the instrument <em>does</em> move when the arithmetic is reorganised, by about two
            parts in ten thousand. <span class="font-bold">Doubling the thread count and moving
            the work from the A78 cluster to the A55 cluster moved it by zero.</span>
          </p>
        </div>
        <CaveatCard title="Boundary">
          <ul class="list-disc pl-4 space-y-1.5">
            <li>Not a claim of bit-exactness: the per-chunk series prints to four decimals, so "identical" means agreement at that resolution. The batch control shows a ~2e-4 relative perturbation is visible here, not that a smaller one would be.</li>
            <li>Greedy decoding only. Above temperature 0 the sampler's RNG dominates and the question changes.</li>
            <li>One device, one model, one quantization, 12 chunks. Only the naive arm has a genuinely different reduction width; the other two share Auto's four threads and differ in placement alone.</li>
          </ul>
        </CaveatCard>
        <p class="text-[0.8125rem] text-dim">
          Runner <code>quant-lab/equivalence.sh</code>, raw outputs
          <code>quant-lab/results/equivalence/</code>, full write-up
          <code>quant-lab/RESULTS.md</code>.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="portability" data-reveal class="mx-auto max-w-[1200px] px-4 md:px-6 py-10 scroll-mt-20">
    <p class="microlabel">The iOS control</p>
    <h2 class="mt-2 text-xl font-bold">How much of this is Arm the architecture, and how much is Arm the platform?</h2>
    <div class="mt-4 grid grid-cols-1 lg:grid-cols-2 gap-4 items-start">
      <div class="text-[0.8125rem] leading-relaxed space-y-3">
        <p>
          Every optimization here works by <em>reading and steering the hardware</em> - which cores
          are fast, what they are clocked at, where a thread lands, what the battery is drawing.
          So: is that about arm64, or about Android exposing arm64?
        </p>
        <p>
          There is a control, and it is the best one available. <span class="font-bold">Apple
          silicon is also arm64</span> - same architecture, same big.LITTLE shape, same class of
          quantized model, very fast. It simply will not let an application see or steer any of it.
        </p>
        <div class="scroll-x card">
          <table class="data-table min-w-[520px]">
            <caption class="sr-only">ENTITY's ten optimization mechanisms on Apple silicon</caption>
            <thead>
              <tr><th scope="col">Mechanism</th><th scope="col">On Apple silicon</th></tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td>Live per-core frequency</td><td>no <code>/sys</code>, no public API - <span class="font-bold">gone</span></td></tr>
              <tr><td>ADPF deadline hints</td><td>no equivalent - <span class="font-bold">gone</span></td></tr>
              <tr><td>Energy telemetry</td><td>no battery current API - <span class="font-bold">gone</span></td></tr>
              <tr><td>Multi-variant v8.0-v9.2 dispatch</td><td>cannot load code outside the bundle - <span class="font-bold">gone</span></td></tr>
              <tr><td>Core placement</td><td><code>sched_setaffinity</code> absent on Darwin; becomes a QoS request that cannot be read back - <span class="font-bold">degraded</span></td></tr>
              <tr><td>Thermal detail</td><td>a temperature becomes four coarse levels - <span class="font-bold">degraded</span></td></tr>
              <tr><td>Thread count</td><td>P-core count reported directly, no heuristic needed - <span class="font-bold">better</span></td></tr>
              <tr><td>KleidiAI advisor</td><td>pure logic - <span class="font-bold">ports</span></td></tr>
              <tr><td>Adaptive context</td><td>a better input exists; the port does not yet use it - <span class="font-bold">ports</span></td></tr>
              <tr><td>Model-fit catalog</td><td>ports, but iOS memory limits are hard and per-app - <span class="font-bold">ports</span></td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p>
          <span class="font-bold">Four cease to exist outright; two survive only degraded.</span>
          A QoS request is not nothing - it is just not verifiable, where Android reads the
          applied CPU mask back. And one mechanism gets <em>better</em>: Apple reports the
          P-core count exactly, where Android has to parse per-core max frequency and count
          what lands within 10% of the fastest - a heuristic this project has already had to
          correct once. The asymmetry is about steering and observing, not capability.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="space-y-4">
        <div class="text-[0.8125rem] leading-relaxed space-y-3">
          <p class="font-bold">Then measured it - real int8 ONNX inference, two iPhones</p>
          <div class="scroll-x card">
            <table class="data-table min-w-[460px]">
              <caption class="sr-only">Thread sweep and KleidiAI on Apple silicon</caption>
              <thead>
                <tr><th scope="col">Device</th><th scope="col" class="num">1 thread</th><th scope="col" class="num">2 threads</th><th scope="col" class="num">KleidiAI</th></tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                <tr><td>iPhone 16 (A18)</td><td class="num">168.8 ms</td><td class="num">197.1 ms (+16.8%)</td><td class="num">1.089&times;</td></tr>
                <tr><td>iPhone 17 Pro Max (A19 Pro)</td><td class="num">179.3 ms</td><td class="num">199.7 ms (+11.4%)</td><td class="num">1.045&times;</td></tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
          <p>
            The runtime settles on <span class="font-bold">one</span> thread and reports
            <code>affinity: unavailable</code>. On Android the thread count is the single largest
            earner in this project. Here a second thread is a straight loss, on both devices -
            because there is no way to say <em>which</em> core it may use. The optimization does
            not fail because the hardware differs. It fails because the platform will not let you
            name a core.
          </p>
          <p>
            Both phones report <span class="font-bold">SME and SME2</span>, extensions none of the
            nine Android SoCs here have. It changes nothing: one static binary, no variant to
            select.
          </p>
          <p>
            And the cheaper phone wins, then loses. The base iPhone 16 leads at peak - 76.7 against
            74.0 tok/s - then gives up <span class="font-bold">11% over six sustained windows</span>
            while the Pro Max holds flat at 1.002&times;. Peak and sustained are different products;
            only one is on the spec sheet.
          </p>
        </div>
        <CaveatCard title="Boundary">
          <ul class="list-disc pl-4 space-y-1.5">
            <li>Different runtime and model from the Android record - ONNX Runtime int8 translation, not llama.cpp GGUF. The two sets are never compared numerically, and no table here puts them side by side.</li>
            <li>No energy numbers. iOS exposes no battery current or voltage, so tokens-per-watt stays an Android-only claim. A separate SwiftUI benchmark emits a power column that is a hardcoded constant per arm; it is published as raw data and quoted nowhere.</li>
            <li>Two devices, one inference workload. A third ONNX data point would strengthen the thread-sweep result, which rests on two devices agreeing.</li>
          </ul>
        </CaveatCard>
        <p class="text-[0.8125rem] text-dim">
          Full analysis <code>docs/PORTABILITY-ARM-VS-APPLE-SILICON.md</code>, raw exports
          <code>benchmarks/results/ios/</code>, iOS sources and builds <code>ios/</code>.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="competition" data-reveal class="mx-auto max-w-[1200px] px-4 md:px-6 py-10 scroll-mt-20">
    <h2 class="text-xl font-bold">Against the competition - both sessions, because they disagree</h2>
    <p class="mt-2 text-dim text-[0.875rem] max-w-[68ch]">
      Same phone (CMF Phone 1), same <code>Llama-3.2-1B-Instruct-Q4_0</code>, same PP 512 / TG
      128, each app's own benchmark screen. Current session 2026-07-20: all three apps
      re-measured the same day, five runs each, 30-minute cooldown between apps.
    </p>
    <div class="mt-4 grid grid-cols-1 lg:grid-cols-2 gap-4 items-start">
      <div>
        <div class="scroll-x card">
          <table class="data-table min-w-[460px]">
            <caption class="sr-only">Three-app comparison, 2026-07-20 session</caption>
            <thead>
              <tr><th scope="col">App</th><th scope="col" class="num">Prompt</th><th scope="col" class="num">Decode</th><th scope="col">Threads</th></tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td>PocketPal AI</td><td class="num">88.32</td><td class="num">13.9</td><td>6</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Arm AI Chat (Arm's own app)</td><td class="num">121 ± 2.99</td><td class="num">12.4 ± 0.08</td><td>not reported</td></tr>
              <tr class="font-bold"><td>ENTITY</td><td class="num">128</td><td class="num">18.2</td><td>4, pinned</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p class="mt-3 text-[0.8125rem]">
          Against Arm's own reference app, on Arm's own silicon: <span class="font-bold">+6%
          prompt, +47% decode</span>. Against PocketPal: +45% and +31%. Decode is where the
          thread-count policy acts; the prompt column is close because all three apps run Q4_0
          and reach the same KleidiAI kernels.
        </p>
        <CaveatCard title="The repeat did not agree - and that is the point">
          <p>
            The same three apps measured on 2026-07-14 read differently: PocketPal and Arm
            swapped places on decode (Arm led 12.9 to 10.9 then; PocketPal leads 13.9 to 12.4
            now), ENTITY's prompt margin over Arm narrowed from +11% to +6%, and its decode
            margin widened from +21% to +47%. PocketPal's decode swung about 27% between
            sessions on identical hardware while Arm's held within about 4% - and that swing is
            what flipped the ranking. <strong>A figure from one session must never be paired
            with a figure from another:</strong> the most inflated pairing available here reads
            +67% where the matched figure is +31%. Both sessions are published with their dates;
            neither is deleted.
          </p>
        </CaveatCard>
      </div>
